A||||20||lowCG||O2 sensor mixture reading||||1||
Z||000000||lowCG||07-23-2000||10:06 PM||||Does anyone know what Mv values exist for for determining the current mixture on an injected engine?<BR>Terry,you mentioned that 850Mv and less was on the lean side,I always thought that the hotter the sensor got,the higher the output,and doesn't a lean engine produce a hotter exhaust?||||reg||1
Z||000001||BBBAD GT||07-23-2000||10:46 PM||||I'm not sure about GM, but a Ford O2 sensor reads 0.00v lean and 1.00v rich, with around .60v being optimal (so I've been told).  But honestly, tring to get a real A/F ratio from stock O2 sensors off WOT is almost impossible.  The computer swings the mixture back and forth from lean to rich trying to get the optimal A/F ratio.  And even at WOT, I wouldn't trust the O2 sensors, they aren't labatory grade like wide ban O2 sensor is, or not as specific as an EGT meter.  You can always do the old fashion 'plug readings' right after a WOT run shutting it down and coasting to a stop.<P>Sorry to get off track.....<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A><BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0's Web Site</A><p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 07-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||07-23-2000||10:50 PM||||The O2 used on the Fiero and most GM cars only knows what 14.7:1 is. It's around .45V depending on the bias to the sensor. When below, it's leaner and above it's richer, but how much is not known. It's not linear. That's why led AFR meters are a nice indictor but not accurate.<P>Considering this .85V is considered the lowest you should go on the O2 if an EGT is not available. It's around 12.5:1, but it will vary from O2 to O2. Until I got an EGT, I would measure three O2's at WOT and use the one that read the lowest (margin of safety).<P>The closed loop operation of the GM computers is called a bang-bang circuit. It goes rich and lean to try and get the O2 to switch evenly across stoich (14.7:1). The overall AFR then averages out to that amount.<P>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||07-23-2000||11:10 PM||||Thanks guys,I know it's running lean because when I let back on the throttle after nearly flooring,it'll surge at that moment,using the fuel that was just sent from the throttle action.<BR>It doesn't seem like it's too far off,however,the car will heat up under load at freeway speeds,and I need to find a way to get some more fuel under load.The fuel pressure responds nice,popping right up to 50 psi,am wondering if an adjustable regulator would be better than a 0-300 ohm pot in the coolant sensor circuit?<BR>This thing wants to go fast,big brakes will be needed real soon,as I smell smoking pads often on the freeway <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
