Z||000009||lowCG||07-24-2000||10:27 PM||||Well,the extra resistance in the temp circuit helped,now just need to go to the electronics store,and get one that has more adjustment,and hope that it will still lean out on the top end.<BR>If not,after a week of fussing,maybe SDS will be getting a phone call soon. <A HREF="http://www.sdsefi.com" TARGET=_blank>www.sdsefi.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||07-24-2000||11:06 PM||||1652 - Fan 2 circuit output<BR>1655 - CCP solenoid or Hot lamp output<BR>1551 - VSS or idle control failure<BR>1810 - Transmission solenoids<P>OBDII can be decoded somewhat like this <A HREF="http://www.autotechbulletinboard.com/Obdii/CodesDia.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.autotechbulletinboard.com/Obdii/CodesDia.htm</A> <P>On the SDS, I'd think going to a 91-93 16149396 ECM would be cheaper and easier since it supports everything we are doing.<P>Changing the CTS value is just pulling it out of closed loop and popping it back into the open loop AFR vs coolant temp table.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||lowCG||07-25-2000||10:21 PM||||I will try an adjustable fuel pressure regulator(Bell Engineering/Cartech)has a feature that allows the rate of enrichment to be controlled,as well as adjustable pressure(not a big advantage from reading injector flow rates,but maybe enough),and if that doesn't do it,then I'll need to start over with a different ECM I guess.<BR>The trick is going to be to get enough fuel in before the knock sensors kick in and spoil the fun,yet not create a condition where it won't lean out again.<BR>The idle mixture is messed up,as well as it being in open loop ,like you said,with the altered temp circuit,but it runs better overall(not really saying much,since it ran lousy before).<BR>Thanks a bunch for the code info also Terry,just noticed that the 1551 is really a 1651,an output of some sort,hopefully not significant.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 07-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||07-25-2000||10:29 PM||||There is no default high idle for a DTC code. You have a dead CTS, stuck IAC or a vacuum leak.<P>This is the 93 drive idle curve:<P>TempF RPM<BR> -40  1700<BR> -18  1600<BR>   3  1400<BR>  24  1350<BR>  46  1300<BR>  68  1250<BR>  90  1200<BR> 111  1150<BR> 132  1000<BR> 154   900<BR> 176   800<BR> 198   800<BR> 219   800<BR> 241   838<BR> 262   875<BR> 284   900<BR> 304   900<P>If the CTS is hosed, you can see what will happen.       <P><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||07-26-2000||12:54 AM||||The first thing I'll do after adding a mechanical temp guage ,is to check out that CTS circuit in the ranges in question,then maybe replace the IAC if I don't find anything really wrong there.<BR>It did something funny tonight after warming up,and idling down,I turned it off for a few seconds,maybe a minute,then when I fired it up again,it idled at over 2K.<BR>Definately will be checking that CTS circuit,thanks again.<BR>I also might try to assign another gear(1st) instead of D,since that may be causing a delayed response.||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||07-26-2000||01:08 AM||||Gremlins. Seen it before.<P>Brake booster leaking? CCP? <P><BR>Did the scanner show a normal coolant temp?<P>TPS working?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||07-26-2000||09:28 AM||||My scanner only displays the code,so I'll need to go get a mechanical-type temp guage to check against the factory one.<BR>So far,no TPS codes,but I'll play around with the ohm meter and see if anything acts funny.Also will check again for any vacume leaks.<BR>Would being in open loop allow the idle speed to rise to 2K?||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||07-26-2000||12:09 PM||||That's a loaded question, but no. Even the idle table doesn't go that high. Of course if you are using the dash tach it could be off by 300 RPM. <P>I would stay with the simple solutions until they are exhausted. Brake booster/hose, standard vacuum leaks. <P>No TPS error so that's not it. The AC idle offset won't send it that high nor will with park/neutral offset. IAT/MAT has little control. Could be related to the DIS, but that would take a major error in the crank trigger.<P>What did you do about the second O2 (I believe it used a post cat O2)? I doubt that would cause it though.<P>I would seriously consider switching to 91-93 ECM. At least it is something you can work with.<P>Terry<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||lowCG||07-26-2000||08:35 PM||||Got the car to stop everheating,still have to replace the water pump now from the hose-blowing incident last weekend as it's become noisy.<BR>It turned out to be the cover I put over the opening under the nose,never overheated at all with the old motor.<BR>It idles better now after spraying some contact cleaner in the TPS,staying at around 1200rpm most of the time.<BR>I was thinking of inputting the signal from the first O2 sensor into the second to see if that would help,but I'm thinking it operates in a different range perhaps.It's funny I don't get a "heater circuit open" error from the second O2 sensor.<BR>I also assigned first gear and cleared up a big stumble,now it just feels like the old L-Jetronic cars did before tightening the spring inside the airflow meter on a car with a large exhaust.The guys at Cartech mentioned a company,or product that's plugged into the airflow meter,inline with the plug,that's supposed to be able and alter the signal somehow,not sure exactly what it's supposed to do though(delay,richen,lean-out).It was something like Split Second Racing,and the product was sold through Summit Racing's catalogue they said.<BR>I do know that the rev-limiter must be raised another 1K (at least),there's no reason a balanced,forged crank engine shouldn't be able and get the most out of the ranges it works best at.<BR>Just might have to get an early ECM,and go for the gusto.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||07-26-2000||10:10 PM||||The first O2 expects to see transitions. The second expects to not see transistion. You might be able to get away with an O2 connected to the wire but not in the exhaust stream.<P>Let me know if you decide to go to the earlier ECM. I have that worked out.||||reg||1
